title: Source Control
files:
  - em.prog:em.examples.basic/Ex02_BlinkerP.em.ts
  - .assets/img-100-04-01.png

steps:
  - cmds:
      - open 1
      - mark 1 A 12
    focus: [1, 1]
    acts: ["$reveal", "$build"]
    text: |

      {[ht,$start]&thinsp;Experimenting with EM&bull;Script}

      Going forward, we'll regularly encourage you to not only explore **EMS** content but *modify* it as well.

      <div class="em-info">

      Don't you want to change that constant on line {[bm,A]} ??

      </div>

      This tour therefore recommends some "best practices" using **Git** source control to sandbox your own work.

      Once in place, you can start experimenting 🧪&thinsp;&hellip;


  - cmds:
      - view 2
    text: |

      {[ht,rebase]&thinsp;Source Control &ndash; A Branch for Work}

      Initially running the <code>git clone&thinsp;&hellip;</code> command from your shell has defined a relation between a pair of repos:

      {[DC,1]} a *remote* `emporium` repo housed at **GitHub**<br>
      {[DC,2]} a *local* `emporium` repo residing on your PC

      Repo {[dc,2]} typically refers to repo {[dc,1]} as its `origin` &ndash; with its local `main` branch automatically tracking `origin:main`.

      <div class="em-info">

      `origin:main` holds the latest *stable* `emporium` release.

      </div>

      From here, you'll enter one essential command at the shell in&shy;side the `~/path/to/emporium` folder on your PC:

      ```bash
          git switch -c work
      ```

      And voilà &ndash; you now have a local sandbox name `work`&thinsp;&hellip;

  - cmds:
      - view 2
    text: |
      {[ht,rebase]&thinsp;Source Control &ndash; Commit or Discard}

      a

  - cmds:
      - view 2
    text: |
      {[ht,rebase]&thinsp;Source Control &ndash; Push&thinsp;&middot;&thinsp;Pull&thinsp;&middot;&thinsp;Merge}

  - cmds:
      - view 2
    text: |

      {[ht,rebase]&thinsp;Source Control &ndash; Contributing Content}

